---
import DocsLayout from "../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Migration"
  description="Move a MyPaaS installation to a fresh Linux VM using the supported backend migration workflow."
>
  <section>
    <p class="overline">Server moves</p>
    <h2>Use the Admin migration workflow</h2>
    <p>
      The supported export path is <strong>Admin → Platform settings → VM Migration</strong>.
      MyPaaS coordinates storage preflight, runtime quiescing, archive creation, and runtime restoration
      before it marks a migration package ready.
    </p>
    <aside class="warning">
      <p>
        Do not treat legacy standalone migration scripts as an alternative supported exporter.
        The old <code>scripts/migrate-export.sh</code> and <code>scripts/migrate-to-podman.sh</code>
        entry points are fail-closed compatibility stubs.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What the exporter verifies</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Storage preflight.</strong> Before stopping runtimes, MyPaaS checks Compose storage.
        If a project uses engine-managed volumes that the built-in migration flow cannot safely carry,
        export fails closed and reports the affected volumes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Quiesce supported runtimes.</strong> Running Dockerfile, registry-image, and Compose
        runtimes are stopped temporarily without changing their desired running state in PostgreSQL.
        Static projects have no application runtime to stop.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Export supported state.</strong> The backend creates logical PostgreSQL dumps and archives
        the host-managed project data supported by the migration contract.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Restore source runtimes.</strong> Every runtime stopped by the exporter is started again.
        The package is marked ready only after runtime restoration succeeds.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <aside class="note"><p>Container-backed projects have a maintenance window while mutable data is exported.</p></aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Engine-managed volumes are a portability boundary</h2>
    <p>
      The built-in exporter does not silently copy arbitrary Docker or Podman engine-managed Compose volumes.
      When preflight reports named or other engine-managed volumes, move persistent data to supported bind mounts
      under <code>/var/lib/mypaas/volumes</code> or migrate those engine volumes separately before relying on the
      built-in VM package.
    </p>
    <p>
      A successful migration package therefore means the supported preflight and export contract passed. It is not
      a claim that every possible container-engine-local storage object is automatically portable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Restore on the destination VM</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>Prepare the migration package from the Admin migration page.</li>
      <li>Wait until the backend reports the package as ready.</li>
      <li>Copy the generated restore command. Its package URL is token-protected and temporary.</li>
      <li>Run that command on a fresh supported Linux VM. It clones MyPaaS and passes the migration package URL to <code>scripts/install-vm.sh</code> for restore.</li>
      <li>Verify the dashboard, control-plane health, project inventory, routes, data, and recovered workloads after the move.</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/nabilrn/MyPaas.git mypaas &amp;&amp; cd mypaas \
  &amp;&amp; bash scripts/install-vm.sh --migrate-url "&lt;package-url-from-dashboard&gt;"</code></pre>
    <aside class="warning">
      <p>
        The migration URL provides access to platform state. Treat it like a secret and use it only on the intended destination VM.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Docker to Podman is not an in-place switch</h2>
    <p>
      Fresh supported hosts are Podman-first, but MyPaaS does not support replacing a stateful Docker Engine
      installation with Podman in place. Engine-local container and named-volume storage is not made portable
      merely by changing the Docker-compatible socket.
    </p>
    <p>
      If changing engine families, use a fresh destination host and the supported VM migration/export path,
      while handling any engine-managed volumes reported by preflight separately.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>